nip">Mstislav Davydovich was prince of Smolensk from 1219 to 1230.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-i-of-kiev"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mstislav-i-of-kiev.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mstislav I of Kiev</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mstislav I, called the Great, was Grand Prince of Kiev from 1125 to 1132, the eldest son of Vladimir Monomakh and Gytha of Wessex.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-ii-of-kiev"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mstislav-ii-of-kiev.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mstislav II of Kiev</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mstislav II of Kiev (Mstislav Iziaslavich) was prince of Volhynia, which he ruled as a separate principality from 1154, and grand prince of Kiev from 1167 to 1169.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-mstislavich"><span class="enc-name">Mstislav Mstislavich</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mstislav Mstislavich, called the Bold (Udatny), was a Rus' prince who ruled Novgorod at intervals from 1210 to 1217 and later Galicia.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mstislav-of-tmutarakan"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mstislav-of-tmutarakan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mstislav of Tmutarakan</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mstislav of Tmutarakan was a son of Vladimir the Great who ruled the principality of Tmutarakan on the Taman peninsula.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mstislavl"><span class="enc-name">Mstislavl</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mstislavl is a small town in Mogilev Region, eastern Belarus, on the Vikhra river in the Sozh basin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mswati-ii"><span class="enc-name">Mswati II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mswati II was king of the Swazi from 1840 to 1868, and the Swazi people take their name from him.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mtamvuna-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mtamvuna-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mtamvuna River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mtamvuna is a river of South Africa that flows into the Indian Ocean and forms part of the boundary between the provinces of KwaZulu-Natal and the Eastern Cape.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mthatha-river"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mthatha-river.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mthatha River</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mthatha is a river of the Eastern Cape in South Africa, flowing south-east past the city of Mthatha into the Indian Ocean.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mthethwa-paramountcy"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mthethwa-paramountcy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mthethwa Paramountcy</span><span class="enc-type">State</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mthethwa Paramountcy was a Nguni state near the coast of what is now KwaZulu-Natal, which under Dingiswayo dominated neighbouring chiefdoms, including the Zulu, in…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/mtskheta"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mtskheta.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mtskheta</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Mtskheta is a small town north of Tbilisi in Georgia, where the Aragvi meets the Kura.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/mu-of-balhae"><span class="enc-name">Mu of Balhae</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mu, personal name Dae Muye, was the second king of Balhae, reigning from 719 to 737, and son of its founder Dae Joyeong.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muallaqat"><span class="enc-name">Mu'allaqat</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Mu'allaqat, or Suspended Odes, are a collection of seven (in some versions ten) long pre-Islamic Arabic poems regarded as the finest of early Arabic poetry.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muayyad-al-dawla"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muayyad-al-dawla.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mu'ayyad al-Dawla</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mu'ayyad al-Dawla was a Buyid ruler, a son of Rukn al-Dawla, who governed Isfahan from 976 as a loyal ally of his brother Adud al-Dawla.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-muayyad-muhammad"><span class="enc-name">al-Mu'ayyad Muhammad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Mu'ayyad Muhammad was the Zaydi imam of Yemen from 1620 to 1644 and the second ruler of the Qasimid State.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-muazzam-isa"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-muazzam-isa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">al-Mu'azzam Isa</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">al-Mu'azzam Isa was the Ayyubid ruler of Damascus from 1218 to 1227, a son of al-Adil and nephew of Saladin.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muin-al-din-suleyman-pervane"><span class="enc-name">Mu'in al-Din Suleyman Pervane</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mu'in al-Din Suleyman, known by his title Pervane, was the leading statesman of the Seljuk Sultanate of Rum in the 1260s and 1270s, governing under Mongol overlordship.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muin-al-din-unur"><span class="enc-name">Mu'in al-Din Unur</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mu'in al-Din Unur was a Turkish mamluk commander who governed Damascus as atabeg for the last Burid rulers from about 1138 until his death in 1149.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-dawla"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/muizz-al-dawla.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Mu'izz al-Dawla</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mu'izz al-Dawla was the youngest of the three Buyid brothers who built a Daylamite empire in Iran and Iraq.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/muizz-al-din-husayn-kart"><span class="enc-name">Mu'izz al-Din Husayn Kart</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mu'izz al-Din Husayn Kart was the most notable ruler of the Kart dynasty of Herat, reigning from 1331 to 1370.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-muizz-ibn-badis"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-muizz-ibn-badis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Al-Mu'izz ibn Badis</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Mu'izz ibn Badis was Zirid emir of Ifriqiya from 1016 to 1062.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/munis-al-muzaffar"><span class="enc-name">Mu'nis al-Muzaffar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Mu'nis al-Muzaffar, also called al-Khadim, the Eunuch, was commander-in-chief of the Abbasid army from 908 until his death in 933 and virtual dictator and king-maker of…</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mutadid"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-mutadid.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">al-Mu'tadid</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Mu'tadid was Abbasid caliph from 892 to 902.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/al-mutasim"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/al-mutasim.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">al-Mu'tasim</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Al-Mu'tasim was an Abbasid caliph who reigned from 833 to 842.</span></a></li></ul>
